#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179602 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179740 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6f30993e-b5c7-5d8a-9b27-2aed2c9223e1" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Sign of a Circulation From a Diagram"}] :question/id #uuid "6c8b136c-7944-599f-9399-19641212fcbe" :question/math-academy-id "e-9537" :question/problem "![](images/f7/f7e61f72ed7488fd490f42ff519316ed2e87d3c997d64e2cc6b7af36f951dbb5.png)\n\nConsider the circle $S$ and vector field $\\mathbf F,$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $S$ is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\displaystyle\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r =  0$\n\n- $\\displaystyle\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r > 0$\n\n- $\\displaystyle\\oint_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r > 0$" :question/worked-solution "The circulation $\\Gamma$ measures the total work done by a force $\\mathbf F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\Gamma = \\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm{d} \\mathbf r\n= \\int_a^b \\mathbf F(r(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf r'(t) \\; \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe now recall the following:\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then $\\displaystyle\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r > 0.$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then $\\displaystyle\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r <                               0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. Since the force $\\mathbf F$ acts perpendicular to the motion of the particle as it moves around $C,$ it neither helps nor hinders its motion. \n\n![](images/06/063836a4780ecb4be6e8c6149a422e3c8e9f2770304e659a8116691b91e3d7f1.png)\n\nSo, $\\displaystyle\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r = 0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. If we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then the situation with regards to the work done by $\\mathbf F$ remains unchanged.\n\n![](images/70/704915d2852e1654aa31d17bd2dfb07a3022fe3d8b597f37bf98bd5af2de1d1b.png)\n\nSo, $\\displaystyle\\oint_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r = 0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179603 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179741 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "976e05d4-59a8-5a93-be2f-b22357f388b9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Circulation"}] :question/id #uuid "16605c7c-c238-51ef-84e6-d5b5d58e1539" :question/math-academy-id "e-9539" :question/problem "Calculate the circulation $\\Gamma$ of the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y) = -y\\,\\mathbf{i} +x\\,\\mathbf{j}$ along the circle $C$ with equation $x^2 + y^2 = 1,$ oriented counterclockwise." :question/worked-solution "The closed curve $C$ is the unit circle. It can be parameterized in the counterclockwise direction as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\cos{t}\\,\\mathbf{i} + \\sin{t}\\,\\mathbf{j},\n\\qquad 0 \\leq t \\lt 2\\pi.\n$$\n\nThe circulation $\\Gamma$ measures the total work done by a force $\\mathbf F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\Gamma = \\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm{d} \\mathbf r\n= \\int_a^b \\mathbf F(r(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf r'(t) \\; \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx = \\cos t, \\qquad\ny = \\sin t.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))=-y\\,i+x\\,j=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf r'(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j) \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Γ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179605 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179743 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1da220a9-2afc-5b63-a423-a4d2b3de9567" :knowledge-point/title "Circulation in Conservative Vector Fields"}] :question/id #uuid "1e15ae93-0a63-506a-a0ae-b15e035f0494" :question/math-academy-id "e-9543" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y) =( e^y+x)\\,\\mathbf i +(xe^y+y^2)\\,\\mathbf j.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\mathbf F$ is conservative\n\n- $\\displaystyle\\int\\limits_C\\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm d\\mathbf r = 0$ for any piecewise-smooth path $C\\in\\mathbb R^2$\n\n- $\\displaystyle\\oint\\limits_C\\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm d\\mathbf r =0$ for any closed path $C\\in\\mathbb R^2$" :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf F$ is a conservative vector field on $\\mathbb R^2,$ then the circulation $\\Gamma = 0$ for any *closed* path $C\\in\\mathbb R^2,$ that is \n\n$$\n\\Gamma = \\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm{d} \\mathbf r = 0.\n$$\n\nWe notice that $\\mathbf F = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j$ is a vector field with domain $D = \\mathbb R^2.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} =  e^y.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. The vector field $\\mathbf F$ is conservative since the partial derivatives are equal and continuous everywhere on $\\mathbb R^2.$\n\n- Statement II is false, while statement III is true. The result $\\Gamma = 0$ applies to closed curves only.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180021 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179741 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "976e05d4-59a8-5a93-be2f-b22357f388b9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Circulation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9524ff2a-1283-5132-9dd1-0a98f1f77d75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177796} {:answer/id #uuid "b7d1c37c-ed08-552b-a796-cde01d1292f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-50π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177797} {:answer/id #uuid "ca14389b-1f92-5a8f-bc38-9abb3719fca0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-25π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177798} {:answer/id #uuid "1b67dc30-eb2b-5a8a-8f4f-f65258b1cf5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-30π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177799} {:answer/id #uuid "573d7436-f4ea-5d87-9366-a51f17772c97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "50π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177800}] :answer-field/id #uuid "f52d9ac8-f158-5ea9-8173-ab4398665687" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177795} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "493ec38d-6098-515e-a5ad-7028e14e4279" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-25\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369286} {:answer/id #uuid "60467e75-9aeb-5f89-b5ba-0cf99f9bee14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-50\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369287} {:answer/id #uuid "b63af696-8674-5081-a959-eef7b89a95b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "50\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369288} {:answer/id #uuid "75a9f5c7-a760-53e9-ad0a-e67425e54722" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369289} {:answer/id #uuid "d22cce2e-96a1-5175-b7a3-60ee2d75ac84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-30\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369290}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "60467e75-9aeb-5f89-b5ba-0cf99f9bee14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-50\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369287} :answer-field/id #uuid "caad0ef3-4ef7-5f6c-a0d6-18c2861ca703" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369285}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "122d93a7-8517-5ac5-b32a-6a8ca24b241f" :question/math-academy-id "q-125393" :question/problem "Calculate the circulation $Γ$ of the vector field $F(x,y)=y\\,i-x\\,j$ along the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =y\\,i-x\\,j \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j)\\cdot (-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j) \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot (-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+(-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot 5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =-25{sin}^{2}⁡t-25{cos}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =-25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Γ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(-25)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-25t\\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =-25(2\\pi )-0 \\\\  & =-50\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180022 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179740 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6f30993e-b5c7-5d8a-9b27-2aed2c9223e1" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Sign of a Circulation From a Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07a2d901-52dc-580c-b53b-e7be3980d2fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459485} {:answer/id #uuid "7cd85d8b-be1b-575e-80ac-713251f2e060" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459486}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "07a2d901-52dc-580c-b53b-e7be3980d2fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459485} :answer-field/id #uuid "68a91996-588b-523d-920f-19a0c8f2360d" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460609} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "445c0216-e1b1-5dd3-8feb-cc822c3d9a14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459487} {:answer/id #uuid "17e40de4-f646-5021-9351-81097f6cd6a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459488}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "17e40de4-f646-5021-9351-81097f6cd6a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459488} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa5092c7-0f51-5842-8bc5-c41ed85755e5" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460610} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a377412f-eec0-5aeb-b5a0-47d4b28ce15c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459489} {:answer/id #uuid "872e5857-37ec-5931-83c9-83ec8a9ea82a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459490}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a377412f-eec0-5aeb-b5a0-47d4b28ce15c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459489} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe9a2195-fa09-5aad-adbc-06b68d958d2b" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460611}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1d320327-1d15-55df-b5dd-771fb0fd1831" :question/math-academy-id "q-125394" :question/problem "![](images/27/270e72ebedac3741e1b7086021b16dc0975ae9852d402673c8044a67f8f774fb.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse $E$ and the vector field $F,$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $E$ is traversed once in the **clockwise** direction. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0$\n- ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$\n- ${∮}_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe now recall the following:\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ assists the motion of the particle as it moves clockwise along $C.$ \n\n![](images/db/dbb6dbdfabccf7a94c67781210238c647e6f3a2e428cb79c4debdbff5e124720.png)\n\nSo, $Γ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ works against the motion of the particle as it moves counterclockwise along $C.$\n\n![](images/1c/1c5aa77b0a89e1fcb0782c9d969d3a81bbec47b004f07bd3a53ccd10e623ba6e.png)\n\nSo, $Γ={∮}_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180026 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179743 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1da220a9-2afc-5b63-a423-a4d2b3de9567" :knowledge-point/title "Circulation in Conservative Vector Fields"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f564f77-c024-5353-a9d9-c19914097692" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459503} {:answer/id #uuid "660514de-f28c-53cb-a27c-34d751510c68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459504}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "660514de-f28c-53cb-a27c-34d751510c68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459504} :answer-field/id #uuid "edfbdfc1-8a04-5a73-afb3-039c7c9d5bc1" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460618} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39b0f616-1749-542e-b105-fe3297acb197" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459505} {:answer/id #uuid "39f55c24-bf0b-53af-ba47-2beb4d0758c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459506}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "39f55c24-bf0b-53af-ba47-2beb4d0758c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459506} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e7a67e4-edf9-59f3-a000-81656a4d6fc4" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460619} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33539880-33d2-58c3-8020-27c2bed37c8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459507} {:answer/id #uuid "20852a9c-f055-5c0a-9c0b-3b8d3eff6c12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459508}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "33539880-33d2-58c3-8020-27c2bed37c8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459507} :answer-field/id #uuid "80bbfcb7-86eb-5f41-a1ce-925daa85ec41" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460620}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "76c1a939-3dff-51ed-a845-c6333747802b" :question/math-academy-id "q-125424" :question/problem "Consider the scalar field $f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** $∇f$ is conservative\n- **II.** ${∮}_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$ for any closed path $C\\in {R}^{2}$\n- **III.** ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$ for any piecewise smooth path $C\\in {R}^{2}$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a conservative vector field on ${R}^{2},$ then the circulation $Γ=0$ for any *closed* path $C\\in {R}^{2},$ that is \n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. The vector field $∇f$ is conservative by definition.\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. The result $Γ=0$ applies to closed curves only.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259810 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179741 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "976e05d4-59a8-5a93-be2f-b22357f388b9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Circulation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d33e2ea-8db6-5ef9-af39-e635cdeedbe1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256910} {:answer/id #uuid "33f16301-edab-5602-af96-594f309388a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257243} {:answer/id #uuid "5acaa948-bf60-5767-bf30-561cf7b9dcff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257572} {:answer/id #uuid "8c32b999-d035-5352-94f4-6157ae6afeb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258012} {:answer/id #uuid "b6cefd61-28ba-5230-bf98-2ca2309bbaa3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258373}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "33f16301-edab-5602-af96-594f309388a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257243} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e1c0cb9-e215-5ac0-86e2-2c6dc53f7d21" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259279}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "38a18171-ca9d-5700-9d3e-85fd0dad7304" :question/math-academy-id "q-125455" :question/problem "Calculate the circulation $Γ$ of the vector field $F(x,y)=x\\,i+y\\,j$ along the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =x\\,i+y\\,j \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j)\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Γ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}(\\frac{-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}{2})\\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}(-1+1) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369291 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179741 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "976e05d4-59a8-5a93-be2f-b22357f388b9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Circulation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2433c883-9454-5783-a42b-a158449a6704" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369293} {:answer/id #uuid "da8f4541-beae-50fa-837a-88c5c1315ce2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369294} {:answer/id #uuid "94ddba9c-b5d2-5aad-bbd2-1982a2662332" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369295} {:answer/id #uuid "759a9631-bed4-55f7-ba67-742c0198c7c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369296} {:answer/id #uuid "40433ff5-a4f9-59d7-b1cf-0b7ff6dd7d8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369297}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "40433ff5-a4f9-59d7-b1cf-0b7ff6dd7d8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369297} :answer-field/id #uuid "e70cc117-b909-56f1-8961-8a545df9549f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369292}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "15f53228-e106-54bd-8baa-01a5e8c54996" :question/math-academy-id "q-125484" :question/problem "Calculate the circulation $Γ$ of the vector field $F(x,y)=2x\\,i+2y\\,j$ along the ellipse $C$ with equation ${x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}=9$ oriented counterclockwise." :question/worked-solution "First, we write the equation of our curve as\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{3}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{1}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nThe closed curve $C$ is the ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal and vertical semiaxes $3$ and $1,$ respectively. It can be parameterized in the counterclockwise direction as\n\n$$\nr(t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nThe circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =2x\\,i+2y\\,j \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j)\\cdot (-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j) \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot (-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =-16\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Γ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }-16\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-8{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-8{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-8(\\frac{-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}{2})\\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =-4(-1+1) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180028 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179741 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "976e05d4-59a8-5a93-be2f-b22357f388b9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Circulation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20b9603d-bac9-510b-8adf-4811f7de36f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257050} {:answer/id #uuid "26ec99ce-8a59-5b3e-a5e2-b2a4376ef85f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257119} {:answer/id #uuid "35ecf7ed-f11b-5da5-add8-dae837fcadb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257256} {:answer/id #uuid "583e6e7c-9d42-5175-a79d-4384af96a328" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257546} {:answer/id #uuid "e914a80c-0913-59c3-b9ed-6b86107e11f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258800}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "26ec99ce-8a59-5b3e-a5e2-b2a4376ef85f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257119} :answer-field/id #uuid "c32e678d-b7b8-57d6-98b9-3d11c1c55626" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259372}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "81113ee4-2843-5316-a3de-8f3bdbd20c2b" :question/math-academy-id "q-125485" :question/problem "Calculate the circulation $Γ$ of the vector field $F(x,y)=\\frac{5}{y}\\,i+\\frac{1}{x}\\,j$ along the ellipse $C$ with equation $25{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=25$ oriented counterclockwise." :question/worked-solution "First, we write the equation of our curve as\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{1}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{5}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nThe closed curve $C$ is the ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal and vertical semiaxes $1$ and $5,$ respectively. It can be parameterized in the counterclockwise direction as\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nThe circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =\\frac{5}{y}\\,i+\\frac{1}{x}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,i+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,i+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,j)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\cdot 5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =-1+5 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Γ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }4\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4t\\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =4(2\\pi )-0 \\\\  & =8\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259829 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179741 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "976e05d4-59a8-5a93-be2f-b22357f388b9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Circulation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37f44aac-0775-51da-a8c4-dfe7f092aa84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257274} {:answer/id #uuid "8c3d44ff-6ba4-5561-aab3-b14b7d6bc9c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258014} {:answer/id #uuid "a9e19bb9-c4fe-5782-9083-dfe133debc1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258264} {:answer/id #uuid "b0ba4ba4-edf7-5f3f-bdb9-f225398b9147" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258321} {:answer/id #uuid "d04d9343-8b1e-53d0-82f6-5c60b51e7eb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258589}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b0ba4ba4-edf7-5f3f-bdb9-f225398b9147" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258321} :answer-field/id #uuid "50d05e08-dfe6-58de-ae50-7ffb1990e442" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259173}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "205601cc-6f43-54e3-8d21-d33b726485ab" :question/math-academy-id "q-125585" :question/problem "Calculate the circulation $Γ$ of the vector field $F(x,y)=\\frac{3}{y}\\,i-\\frac{2}{x}\\,j$ along the ellipse $C$ with equation $9{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=36$ oriented counterclockwise." :question/worked-solution "First, we write the equation of our curve as\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{3}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nThe closed curve $C$ is the ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal and vertical semiaxes $2$ and $3,$ respectively. It can be parameterized in the counterclockwise direction as\n\n$$\nr(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nThe circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =\\frac{3}{y}\\,i-\\frac{2}{x}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,i-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,i-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,j)\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\cdot 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =-2-3 \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Γ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(-5)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-5t\\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =-5(2\\pi )-0 \\\\  & =-10\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259834 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179740 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6f30993e-b5c7-5d8a-9b27-2aed2c9223e1" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Sign of a Circulation From a Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a074685c-6f81-5431-86de-5f20c4658e9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463488} {:answer/id #uuid "24e36323-76da-51e5-bfb2-592610002fa1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463489}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "24e36323-76da-51e5-bfb2-592610002fa1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463489} :answer-field/id #uuid "c743c6e7-059d-5909-9335-e5ad04088599" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464678} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6209e29c-6f4f-5cd2-b854-a9a719c264fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463490} {:answer/id #uuid "a906d4ee-e6aa-54de-9527-ace41765eb04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463491}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a906d4ee-e6aa-54de-9527-ace41765eb04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463491} :answer-field/id #uuid "8fdabed2-ee70-5863-b4f7-2f54d7e967e7" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464679} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13acff26-2b2e-5369-9c05-1a4ae2971db2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463492} {:answer/id #uuid "21155136-2b73-54fb-aa55-8041c9107e12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463493}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "13acff26-2b2e-5369-9c05-1a4ae2971db2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463492} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d92b4b5-1cec-55de-8dcb-2ff6ec78d476" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464680}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "547c79d0-6e8c-5db1-9245-a09abf6b78b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-125637" :question/problem "![](images/e6/e60f7c0adc3e2ec8daa3203564c31bf660e6eca7e6394955cc73133ac3673ed5.png)\n\nConsider the circle $S$ and the vector field $F,$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $S$ is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$\n- ${∮}_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0$\n- ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe now recall the following:\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement III is false. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ assists the motion of the particle as it moves counterclockwise along $C.$ \n\n![](images/a8/a8010db8481768cb61d0734dcfc97eed646441ab265c4928d06d274ef0e1c1dd.png)\n\nSo, ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\n- Statement II is true. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ works against the motion of the particle as it moves **clockwise** along $C.$\n\n![](images/be/be263d706bfc3ef205be37aaed85d4984d3739d53cdaadb8cba88e3d5196ce93.png)\n\nSo, ${∮}_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415312 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179740 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6f30993e-b5c7-5d8a-9b27-2aed2c9223e1" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Sign of a Circulation From a Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "55d9a389-0207-5627-8724-2ab06942a52d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472532} {:answer/id #uuid "8021b753-e25f-51ff-8c8f-fab09d9d89b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472533}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8021b753-e25f-51ff-8c8f-fab09d9d89b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472533} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f990914-9c06-5321-9dbf-d829370622d6" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473661} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7f7147a8-f34e-5218-b7c5-8ea2c830c0bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472534} {:answer/id #uuid "308c82b6-5505-533c-a6a4-f3335f961206" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472535}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "308c82b6-5505-533c-a6a4-f3335f961206" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472535} :answer-field/id #uuid "be5dec92-40f9-56cc-a873-06f88cbc4087" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473662} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "975f0d9b-1723-5cdd-bb95-28c94959e5c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472536} {:answer/id #uuid "8caa6e88-f7bc-5579-83d6-8f86c7a5803f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472537}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "975f0d9b-1723-5cdd-bb95-28c94959e5c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472536} :answer-field/id #uuid "9b8664d6-4e9d-52a7-ac0d-f153ff8d27fa" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473663}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2e8f010b-1d85-5f55-981d-5803f1cc03c7" :question/math-academy-id "q-125645" :question/problem "![](images/98/981d966645eec322afbfe28a5a1f519eeab8b3557692dcce368a87fe748dddd6.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse $E$ and the vector field $F,$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $E$ is traversed once in the **clockwise** direction. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$\n- ${∮}_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0$\n- ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe now recall the following:\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement III is false. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ assists the motion of the particle as it moves clockwise along $C.$ \n\n![](images/b2/b2473eb0c505d75d2c448c1a0d714d05d42737ce8d601401e925834a25ae9e14.png)\n\nSo, $Γ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\n- Statement II is true. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ works against the motion of the particle as it moves counterclockwise along $C.$\n\n![](images/85/8513d9d15d204b6f63f310a37465d6b8dfb21239ca412bac7d3b9936f2bf5ebf.png)\n\nSo, $Γ={∮}_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259837 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179743 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1da220a9-2afc-5b63-a423-a4d2b3de9567" :knowledge-point/title "Circulation in Conservative Vector Fields"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bc049f21-a499-5e1c-bdab-1491bf8bd7ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448194} {:answer/id #uuid "70dba20a-9283-5a64-92e1-e818bbe45555" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448195}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bc049f21-a499-5e1c-bdab-1491bf8bd7ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448194} :answer-field/id #uuid "74956c98-32c5-5a81-9fd6-d30967d81c85" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449137} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7320a730-56b8-5dc9-935e-a931fcfdca97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448196} {:answer/id #uuid "14225c91-d53e-564c-a75b-2ff3a307db5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448197}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7320a730-56b8-5dc9-935e-a931fcfdca97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448196} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4650f15-3ab9-5cbb-8a44-4f88d6ffeef2" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449138} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "080320d7-6a08-5185-be7b-bf365f7873d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448198} {:answer/id #uuid "556f08e3-7136-594b-bdbf-c1887d7ea54c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448199}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "556f08e3-7136-594b-bdbf-c1887d7ea54c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448199} :answer-field/id #uuid "aff3da6e-b916-5bbe-aee8-f8bb72ec84cd" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449139}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "78e40fa2-e07f-5983-b8b1-ed694102f21b" :question/math-academy-id "q-125648" :question/problem "Consider the scalar field $f(x,y)={e}^{y}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $∇f$ is **not** conservative\n- **II.** ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$ for any piecewise linear path $C\\in {R}^{2}$\n- **III.** ${∮}_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$ for any circle $C\\in {R}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a conservative vector field on ${R}^{2},$ then the circulation $Γ=0$ for any *closed* path $C\\in {R}^{2},$ that is \n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. The vector field $∇f$ is conservative by definition.\n\n- Statement II is false, while statement III is true. The result $Γ=0$ applies to closed curves only.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501169 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179740 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6f30993e-b5c7-5d8a-9b27-2aed2c9223e1" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Sign of a Circulation From a Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "083c5ede-cedd-556a-8b65-fe48a6829892" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500481} {:answer/id 